langchain pipeline looks like this:

1- load your data:
everything starts with converting your source data (pdf, webpage, files_extension) into langchain's
standard format called document object!

--> how does document object work?
the langchain document is the universal data carrier that travels through every step of the RAG pipeline

why do we use document object, and not a list or different data structure?
document comes to solve a core problem thats:
    the raw data that we try to index comes in differenet forms:
        pdf, code files, web pages, etc...
        each with different forms
        ==> we need one single obejct that carries the data and enables every component of the  
            pipeline to understand

whats document?

is just a simple container with three fields:
    page_content: str == actual contenet of txt
    metadata: dict == info about text: source = file.pdf, page, author, data. etc../
    id: str (optional) == unique identifier, useful for tracking a specific chunk!

how documetns help?

without document object, you would have to create a list[str] for text/content, list[dict] 
for metadata, and a list[np.ndarray] for vectors.
you'd have to manually keep them aligned through everystep, which is error-prone 

document solves this binding content + metadata into a single unit!
which allows it to travel together!

    
how it works in the pipeline:
1- loading
2- splitting: text_splitter
3- embedding and storing
4- retreival
5- generation












===========================================================================================

2- splitting into chunks:
a full document is usually too large for embeddings to capture meaningingfullly and for the LLM's
context window

so we split into chunks, snippets, so retrieval can be precise

the recommneded splitter is recursivecharactertextersplitter
it tries to split on natural boundaries (paragphras, senteces) and supports overlap

--> recursive, overlap, chunking, chunk_size

1- whats splitting:

splitting is the step where you beakr big documents into smaller ones
this is ciritical for rag:
1- embedding needs focus: 50 pages document becomes a blurry average of meanings
2- retrieval needs precision: you want to find hte paragphra about restting not the whole manual
3- LLM have limited context limits: you can only fit so much text into prompt



a- recursive splitting

recursive spliiting uses a list of seprators:
list_of_separtors = [\n\n. \n. ",". ".", "",  " "]

\n\n == end of paragraph
if too big, move to \n
and so on....

how does recursive handle cutting words:
RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter does NOT cut mid-word if it can help it. 
It looks backward from the 1000-character mark to find the nearest separator that came before it
and cuts there. It never moves forward.


b- overlapping
overlapping came to solve 2 main problems:

1- cutting mid word: wonderful == [wonder] [ful] --> incoherent meaning
2- cutitng mid sentence: hold the button for ten seconds to activate the program
embedding_1 [hold the button for ten]
embedding_w [seconds to activate the program]

==> to retireve the chunk to answer this question: how long should i hold the button
neither the embedding provide an answer, hence retireve the correspeonding chunk of the question is impossible

c- how overlapping solve this:
Chunk 1: ...[hold the button for ten seconds and then]
Chunk 2: [for ten seconds and then release it slowly]...

==> by repeating the boundary check in both chunks, now both chunks complete the idea!
which one you retireve, has an answer!!


d- how does it work?
splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(
    chunk_size=1000,
    chunk_overlap=200
)

chunk_size == 1000, each chunk is at least 1000CHAR LONG
chunk_overlap == each chunk starts at least 200 cgaracter before the previous chunk ended

Text:    [==================================================]
Chunk 1: [==========1000==========]
Chunk 2:                    [==========1000==========]
                            └─200─┘
Chunk 3:                                       [==========1000==========]
                                                └─200─┘


chunk 1 == 1000 character long chunk, but with catering for the mid-word cutting! could be less
chunk 2 == 200 character of the last chunk + 800 new character
chunk 3 == the last 200 character of chunk 2 +  800 new character!

==> this is how our chunks will look like!



















