3- creating embeddings:

embeddings convert each text into a numeric_vector that caputures the semantic meaning
texts taht are similar will have vectors that are close together in vector space


===========================================================================================


4- storing in a vector store:
a vectorstore indexses these embeddings so you can perform similarity search at query time!
it stores mapping between 

LangChain integrates with many vector stores: InMemoryVectorStore for lightweight/prototyping, FAISS for in-memory speed, Chroma for persistence, and PostgreSQL (pgvector) for production databases


===========================================================================================


5-  Retrieving Relevant Context

At query time, you convert the user's question into an embedding using the same embeddings model, then search the vector store for the most similar chunks.

The vector store returns the top k relevant document chunks. These are your snippets of context.


===========================================================================================


6- generating answers:

Now you combine retrieval with generation. You feed the retrieved chunks into an LLM prompt as context, along with the user's question. The LLM answers only based on the provided context—this grounds the response and reduces hallucination



